THE DELETE HALF of Peter's whole-dataset quality pass (ubersitrep, 2026-08-18): "sort by brightness all the images and compare epochs and delete the entirely cloudy ones - a first pass at quality considerations." The measurement half went to astro-science; the retention decision is yours, and there are three objections to weigh before anything is removed.

1. IT RUNS AGAINST YOUR STATED INVARIANT. Your blurb says outright: "Retention is a *resolution schedule*, not keep-vs-delete: recent nights at full cadence, older nights progressively squashed, squashed form kept ~forever (the RRD pyramid -> O(1) storage)." Deleting cloudy nights is keep-vs-delete. The house-pattern alternative is to SQUASH THE CLOUDY NIGHTS HARD rather than delete them — same space win, reversible in kind, consistent with the pyramid. Peter may well want the delete anyway; the point is that it is a deliberate exception to a written rule, not a routine prune.

2. DELETING CLOUDY NIGHTS DESTROYS CALIBRATION DATA THAT IS CURRENTLY BLOCKING astro-science. Its STATE has `sky_clear_max_stops` (11.5) PENDING and "provisional until a genuinely CLOUDY canon night is logged" — bounded from below only. The cloudy nights ARE the missing calibration set. Culling them first would close a gap that has been open since 08-11 and cannot be reopened without waiting for more bad weather. Sequence it the other way: measure and bound scs, THEN cull.

3. SPACE IS NOT THE PRESSURE. bigstore is 5.5T at 27% used, 4.0T free (2026-08-12), and you have already ruled on this shape once: "we don't squash much now because there is more storage... so saving 64G is noise." If the motive is quality-of-dataset rather than space, say so — it changes what "entirely cloudy" has to mean, and it means the cull can be slow and careful rather than urgent.

MECHANICS IF IT GOES AHEAD: `trash`, never `rm` (GLOBAL.md; recoverable 14 days on puppy). Verify the redundancy ledger first — clearing a cloudy night that happens to be one of the 20 nights at < 2.0 weighted copies removes the LAST copy, which is a different act from clearing surplus.

A GOOD THIS-THEN-THAT WITH THE GDRIVE QUESTION spooled earlier today: CULL BEFORE YOU MIRROR. If the quality pass runs first, the ~300-400 GB DR set shrinks by whatever the cloudy fraction is, and you back up only what survived. Doing it in the other order means paying the seed cost twice. This also argues the quality pass is the higher-priority of the two.
